  <section class="wrap alt-extra" aria-labelledby="self-hosted">
    <h2 id="self-hosted">A self-hosted Loom alternative, with your own storage</h2>
    <p>Loom keeps your videos on Loom's cloud. Reelbird's free links go to an S3-compatible bucket you own, and that's the whole setup: Reelbird uploads a small viewer page and the video to the bucket, signed with your keys, so there's no server, database or Docker container to run.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Any S3-compatible storage.</strong> Cloudflare R2 (recommended, because it doesn't charge when people watch), AWS S3, Backblaze B2, or MinIO on your own machine.</li>
      <li><strong>What it costs.</strong> R2's free tier holds 10 GB a month with free egress, a few hours of shared video. Past that, 100 GB is about $1.35 a month (R2 pricing, October 2026).</li>
      <li><strong>Your keys stay on your Mac,</strong> in the Keychain. Nothing goes through Reelbird's servers.</li>
      <li><strong>Unlisted, not secret.</strong> Anyone with a link can watch it. Stop sharing deletes every file under that link.</li>
      <li><strong>From the terminal and CI</strong> too: the <code>reelbird</code> command line tool uses the same settings, or environment variables.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Setup takes about ten minutes, once. Follow <a href="/guides/self-host-screen-recording-links-cloudflare-r2/">self-hosted share links on Cloudflare R2</a>, step by step, or the <a href="/docs/sharing/">sharing docs</a> for S3, B2 and MinIO. If you'd rather not run anything, <a href="/pricing/">Reelbird Pro</a> hosts the links for $5 a month.</p>
    <p>Cap is the other open-source option here. You can self-host its whole server (web app, database, media server and storage) with Docker Compose, and pointing Cap's hosted plan at your own S3 bucket needs Cap Pro (checked 7 November 2026). See <a href="/cap-alternative/">Reelbird vs Cap</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Alt>
